
  The SCO OpenServer 6 port provides these meters:

    - load meter
    - cpu meter:  user | system | io-wait | idle
    - mem meter:  used | free
    - swap meter: used | free

  None of them need any privileges.

  Building
  --------

    gmake

  targets/osr6 builds with gcc; the UDK C++ driver at /udk/usr/ccs/bin/CC
  works too if that is what is installed.  OpenServer ships no libXpm, so
  the target builds with -DNO_XPM, which drops support for the pixmapName
  resource and leaves everything else intact.

  Where the numbers come from
  ---------------------------

  Everything goes through the small interface in osr6/osr6stats.h.  The cpu
  counters and free memory come from the MAS metric file that sar(1) and
  top read, and swap from swapctl(2).

  Two quirks are worth knowing about.  The kernel publishes free memory as
  an accumulator rather than a level, so the mem meter reads zero free for
  its first sample and is correct from the second on.  And it keeps
  freefilemem identical to freemem, so the file cache cannot be shown as a
  field of its own the way it is on other platforms.

  The load meter is not a kernel figure at all: avenrun exists as a symbol
  but is never updated, which is why uptime(1) always prints 0.00.  The
  meter samples the run queue out of /proc instead and decays those samples
  into a 1 minute average itself, so it needs a minute of running before it
  settles.

  Untested
  --------

  This port has not yet been built or run on real hardware.

  OpenServer 5 is not supported: it has no MAS.
